WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.280
How does insertion sort work?

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:03.980
We will sort these six

3
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.040
numbers, derive the time complexity,

4
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.460
and finish with the details

5
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.240
you should remember. Do keep

6
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:09.460
up.

7
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.660
Keep a sorted prefix on

8
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.420
the left. The first element,

9
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.920
one, is already sorted. Take

10
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.460
the next element and move

11
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.368
it left across larger neighbours,

12
00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.100
one adjacent swap at a

13
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:21.440
time.

14
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.200
Start with four. Compare it

15
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.480
with one. One is smaller,

16
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:29.000
so four stays put. The

17
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.140
sorted prefix now contains one

18
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:31.800
and four.

19
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.380
Next, take three. Four is

20
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.240
larger, so swap the adjacent

21
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.460
four and three.

22
00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.380
Compare three with one. One

23
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.700
is smaller, so stop. The

24
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.280
prefix is now one, three,

25
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:47.280
four.

26
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.060
Take six. Four is smaller,

27
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.580
so no swap is needed.

28
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.700
Extend the sorted prefix to

29
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:54.380
include six.

30
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.540
Take five. Six is larger,

31
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.000
so swap six and five.

32
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.360
Four is smaller than five,

33
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.540
so stop. The first five

34
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:05.560
elements are sorted.

35
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.320
Now take two. It must

36
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.900
travel left through the sorted

37
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.940
prefix. Watch each comparison and

38
00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:13.220
swap.

39
00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.580
Six is larger than two.

40
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.760
Swap them, moving two one

41
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.600
position left.

42
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.920
Five is larger than two.

43
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:22.260
Swap again.

44
00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.840
Four is larger than two.

45
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:25.980
Swap again.

46
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.380
Three is larger than two.

47
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.020
Swap once more.

48
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.280
One is smaller than two,

49
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.680
so stop. All six numbers

50
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.048
are sorted. That took eleven

51
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.620
key comparisons and six adjacent

52
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.386
swaps. They are different counts,

53
00:01:41.386 --> 00:01:41.820
darling.

54
00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.160
Why is stopping safe? The

55
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.200
prefix was already sorted, so

56
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.529
everything before that smaller neighbour

57
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.900
is also no larger than

58
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.720
the inserted element. Each insertion

59
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.100
preserves every record and enlarges

60
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.040
the sorted prefix.

61
00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.480
To derive the worst case,

62
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.140
reverse the six numbers. Each

63
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.580
new element is smaller than

64
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.760
the entire prefix. Successive insertions

65
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.840
need one, two, three, four,

66
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:09.960
then five swaps.

67
00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.500
For n elements, add one

68
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.540
through n minus one. The

69
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.080
sum is n times n

70
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.780
minus one, divided by two.

71
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.440
That grows quadratically, giving theta

72
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.400
of n squared worst-case time.

73
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.200
For already sorted input, each

74
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.420
insertion makes one key comparison

75
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.440
and stops. There are n

76
00:02:29.235 --> 00:02:31.340
minus one such comparisons and

77
00:02:31.387 --> 00:02:33.900
zero swaps. The best-case time

78
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.060
is theta of n.

79
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.540
For a uniformly random ordering

80
00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.520
of distinct keys, each pair

81
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.400
is inverted with probability one

82
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.560
half. The expected swap count

83
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.980
is n times n minus

84
00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.580
one, divided by four. Average

85
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.100
time is still quadratic.

86
00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.240
More precisely, an inversion is

87
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.060
an earlier element larger than

88
00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.980
a later one. Every adjacent

89
00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.520
swap removes exactly one inversion.

90
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.200
With I inversions, runtime is

91
00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.800
theta of n plus I.

92
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.360
Few inversions explain why nearly

93
00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:06.900
sorted data can be fast.

94
00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:08.940
Things to take note of.

95
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.920
Swap only when the left

96
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.280
key is strictly greater. On

97
00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.881
equal keys, stop. Two A

98
00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:17.986
must stay before two B,

99
00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.840
so this version is stable.

100
00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.640
Greater than or equal would

101
00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.860
break that guarantee.

102
00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.835
This iterative version is in-place.

103
00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.240
Its indices and one temporary

104
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.540
swap value use constant extra

105
00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.720
space. An empty array or

106
00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.560
a singleton needs no insertion.

107
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.620
The outer loop starts at

108
00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:37.340
index one.

109
00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.580
Binary search can find the

110
00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.780
insertion position with fewer comparisons.

111
00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:43.460
But elements in an array

112
00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:45.400
still have to move. Binary

113
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.620
insertion therefore still has quadratic

114
00:03:48.125 --> 00:03:49.460
worst-case movement and time.

115
00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.240
When tracing, show every insertion

116
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.720
and mark the growing sorted

117
00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.300
prefix. Count key comparisons separately

118
00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.900
from swaps. Remember: linear best

119
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.020
case, quadratic average and worst

120
00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.720
cases, constant extra space, and

121
00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.420
stable with the strict comparison.

122
00:04:05.346 --> 00:04:07.500
Hmph. Your turn to explain

123
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:07.720
it.
